From 127ab77c25478b20abbcdd182f0d8d9610b5d80d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 20:12:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Player fixes 1, 2, 7: count every lost frame, steadier
 pacer, choppy copies
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Finishes the interrupted work of 44faa79.

Fix 1, count every lost frame:
- FramePacer says what it did with each frame (move: next, a refresh
  later, same refresh as the frame before, followed, earlier, start,
  Media3's own times). PacedVideoRenderer counts frames shown twice and
  frames put on the same refresh (FrameCounts).
- FrameGaps counts frames the decoder never gave: gaps in the film's
  times over 1.5 frames, less Media3's dropped and skipped deltas. A
  frame's length is measured (median of the latest 16 gaps); gaps are
  only counted once measured, so a wrongly stated rate can't invent them.
- FrameTally keeps a rolling per-minute tally (Media3 dropped, shown
  twice, same refresh, never decoded, shown), sampled once a second from
  postPlayTick.
- PlaybackDiag (log tag FirePlayerDiag): event lines for the decoder
  chosen and released, picture and sound formats, rebuffer start and end,
  Media3's drop batches; every 10 s while playing, one line with those
  counts (10 s and last minute), buffered ms, the film allocator's bytes,
  the picture (size, fps, codec, HDR type, bits), the decoder, the TV's
  refresh rate and HDR types, Java/native/graphics/total memory
  (Debug.MemoryInfo), availMem/threshold/lowMemory, thermal status (API
  29+) and headroom (API 30+). Read on its own low-priority thread, one
  at a time. Never a link, title or key.
- Picture & sound's "Dropped" row: "Last minute: dropped X · shown twice
  Y · same refresh Z · never decoded W", then Media3's total; refreshed
  once a second while the window is open.

Fix 2, steadier pacer:
- FramePacer.paced keeps an average of 'behind' (alpha 1/16) and moves a
  refresh only when the average passes 0.6 of one, taking the move off
  the average; it is reset wherever lastTargetNs resets.
- A frame 0.9 of a refresh or more from where the frames have been is
  followed at once; frames missing in the film's times (FrameGaps) are
  followed exactly, so a Media3 drop leaves one gap even with jitter.
- Frames are paced by the film's frame length, so a TV at twice or three
  times the film's rate (25 fps on 50 Hz) keeps 2 or 3 refreshes a frame.
- "Same refresh as the frame before" now skips the buffer
  (skipOutputBuffer) instead of queueing one SurfaceFlinger would drop,
  except for a frame Media3 waits on as the first one shown.
- In the model of the research (20,000 frames at 23.976 on 23.976 Hz)
  10-15 ms jitter near half a refresh lost 12.7-17.9% of frames with the
  old rule and 0-0.035% now; 0.1% drift is still corrected one move at a
  time; a Media3 drop gives exactly one gap.

Fix 7, choppy copies:
- CopyHealth gains droppedFrames (every lost kind) and renderedFrames,
  encoded as optional 6th and 7th fields: old 5-field strings still
  decode, and old readers still read the first five. A copy losing more
  than 2% of frames over 10+ minutes is troubled and says "loses N% of
  frames" instead of "smooth". Fed once a second from the tally (frames
  lost in the 3 s after a jump aren't held against the copy).
- Picture & sound's "Range" says "HDR10 · shown as SDR (TV reports no
  HDR)" on a TV without HDR.
- CopyRank on a TV without HDR prefers an SDR copy with at least
  HDR_FLOOR of the best copy's data, gives no HDR/10-bit reasons there,
  and notes "HDR10 shown as SDR on this TV" as a cost.

Tests: FramePacerTest (jitter, drift, drops, twice the rate, moves),
FrameGapsTest, FrameTallyTest, CopyFramesTest, PlaybackDiagTest.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01CeAGQc19KZMJTFxzJrjwSq
